      "side": 1,
      "message": "The idea behind PS2 is not to fix the issue described in the previous comment[1], because I believe that\u0027s not an issue: not considering the `start` function, `mFinished` will only be set to true on the worker thread, and given that we process `Command` one by one on the worker thread in the order they are enqueued, the first `Command` that makes `mProcessor` return `Stop` will effectively prevents any pending `Command`s from being actually processed. So \"3. The worker thread gets the task from the queue and starts processing it (line 147)\" won\u0027t happen if a previous `Command` makes `mProcessor` return `Stop`. It will be either line 163 or 167 in PS1 where we access this newly enqueued command after `mFinished` is set to `true`.\n\nHowever, the proposed approach and PS 1 suffer from such race condition:\n\n1. Thread 0 calls enqueue. Test `mFinished`, find that it\u0027s `false`.\n2. Thread 1, the worker thread, hits a command that returns `Stop` and set `mFinished` to `true`. We then drain both `todo` and `mQueue`, and return.\n3. Thread 0 proceed to push back the task to `mQueue`, ending up returning a `std::future` that will never be set to the ready status.\n\nTherefore, testing `mFinished` and enqueue to `mQueue`, and setting `mFinished` must be under the same critical section. Putting `mQueue` and `mFinished` under the same lock will make a stronger guarantee: after `mFinished` set to true, `mQueue` is empty.\n\nChanged existing logic to get rid of the seemingly redundant code to drain the `todo` task queue. However, I am in favor of keeping the `shouldStop` variable, so that we always write to the `mFinished` variable with the lock held and changing the state of `mQueue` at the same time. WDYT?\n\n[1] https://android-review.googlesource.com/c/platform/hardware/google/aemu/+/2243762/comment/aa04a636_16945f19",
